import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'..')
from con_helpers import *
save(2,5,"Buddhi: The Decider and the Meaning You Give","When a feeling spikes this week, find the verdict buddhi just passed. Then ask three gentle questions: Is that actually true? What else could it mean? What would I tell a friend who believed it?",[
mc("A","You hear a creak downstairs in the dark. One reading is “the house settling” and another is “someone has broken in.” What does Swamiji say this shows?",
 ["The sound is the same; the calm or terror comes from buddhi’s decision about what it means.","The sound is different each time, so the body is reacting to two different events that happen in turn.","The sound is the cause of the terror, so a person should be afraid of any creak that they hear at night.","The sound is not heard at all by manas, which leaves buddhi to supply both the sound and its meaning."],0,
 "Same creak, identical sound waves. Buddhi does three things: resolve what it is, interpret what it means and direct what to do. The middle one is where the emotional weight lives. A friend’s ambiguous remark or a lost job bends a whole year around which meaning was chosen.",
 "It is the meaning, not the event","Part 1"),
mc("A","James’s student yawns, and James feels a sting. What does Swamiji say about where the sting comes from?",
 ["From buddhi’s reading, “they’re bored, I’m failing,” not the yawn: had it said “up late,” no sting.","From the yawn itself, since a yawn in a lesson is a sign of boredom, and a teacher has to take it as one.","From manas, since manas is the worker that attaches a feeling to every signal that it receives and sorts.","From the student, since a student who yawns in class shows a lack of respect, and a teacher feels the slight."],0,
 "Manas: a student yawned. Buddhi: they’re bored, I’m failing. The sting, the defensiveness, hangs off that interpretation. The event is rarely the problem; the meaning is, which is why two people in the same meeting walk out into different realities.",
 "The sting hangs off the interpretation","Part 1"),
mc("U","In the chariot of the Kaṭha Upaniṣad, why does the tradition put buddhi, and not the other workers, in the driver’s seat?",
 ["It governs: the charioteer sees the road and holds the reins, so clearing it brings order.","It is the strongest worker: the horses obey whichever worker pulls hardest, and buddhi pulls hardest.","It is the oldest worker: it was formed first, and the others were formed after it, so it comes first.","It is the quietest worker: it makes no noise, so it is the only one that can be trusted to steer."],0,
 "When buddhi is clear and alert, manas steadies, the senses are engaged but not enslaved, ahaṁkāra reacts proportionately and memory stores clean impressions. When the charioteer is asleep the horses bolt and the rider is dragged along helplessly.",
 "Buddhi is the governing worker","Part 2"),
mc("A","Kai asks why his judgement is clear on some days and terrible on others. How does Swamiji answer with the lake and the moon?",
 ["Buddhi is no fixed lens: still water is sattva, whipped rajas, muddy tamas, each changing the reflection.","Buddhi is a fixed lens, so the bad days are due to outside events and not to anything within him.","Buddhi is a lake that is always still, so the bad days come from the moon, which is the Self, and not from it.","Buddhi is a lake that cannot be changed, so a person has to accept the days as they come and wait."],0,
 "Rajas is the day you know the right call and make the wrong one because the wanting bends every meaning toward what you crave; tamas is fog, with no reflection at all. Rhythm, sleep, simple food, good company and a little meditation are ways of settling the water, not moralising.",
 "The three guṇas change buddhi’s clarity","Part 2"),
judge("“In Vivekananda’s image, people differ in wisdom because the pure light at the centre of the glass globe differs from person to person.”",F,
 "The light is the same in every globe; what differs is the glass, how thick, clouded or coloured. So differences in wisdom are differences in the clarity of the instrument, which can be cleaned, and not differences in deepest worth. Sarah: not broken, just the glass needs clearing.",
 "The light is the same; only the glass differs","Part 2"),
mc("A","Sarah thinks “if my child fails this test, my whole life is over” at 2 a.m., and Kai thinks “either I am perfect or I am worthless.” What does the lesson call these?",
 ["Catastrophising and black-and-white thinking: buddhi misreading the input, not manas missing it.","Failures of attention, since manas has not taken in the input cleanly and has to be steadied.","Failures of memory, since chitta holds the wrong picture and has to be sorted out and cleared.","Failures of will, since the person has not tried hard enough to see things in the right way."],0,
 "CBT’s core discovery is that it is not events that disturb us but the interpretations we place on them: the buddhi principle rediscovered in a clinic. Others are personalising, mind-reading and overgeneralising. Swamiji says you do not need more focus; you need to catch the automatic meaning and choose a truer one.",
 "Buddhi-level distortions","Part 3"),
mc("A","A therapist asks “what is the evidence for that thought?” How does Swamiji describe what this does?",
 ["It is buddhi-training: it clears the reflection, and the traditions then take it further to viveka.","It is manas-training: it steadies the attention, and that is all that the question is able to do.","It is memory-training: it replaces old impressions with new ones, and that is all that the question does.","It is ego-training: it makes the person more certain of their own opinion, whatever the evidence."],0,
 "Where is the evidence, what is another way to read this, what would you tell a friend: each clears buddhi’s reflection. The contemplative traditions go one step further, training buddhi in viveka, the discrimination between the real and the passing states you mistake for you.",
 "The therapist’s question trains buddhi","Part 3"),
mc("U","In the snake and rope image, what is viveka?",
 ["The lamp: it shows the fear was a misreading, and the suffering a formation appearing in awareness.","The snake: it is the fear itself, which is real, and which should be met with courage on the path.","The rope: it is the plain fact, which is hidden, and which is found by looking more closely at it.","The dusk: it is the dim light, which makes the misreading, and which is the cause of the fear."],0,
 "Viveka does not just swap a bad interpretation for a better one. It shows that the suffering was real as an experience but not the final truth of who you are. Humphry Davy’s laughing-gas episode is told as an illustration, not mysticism: the clearer the glass, the more of the light comes through.",
 "Viveka is the lamp","Part 3"),
judge("“Swamiji tells the Humphry Davy story as proof that the material world is only an idea, and asks the group to accept it on that basis.”",F,
 "He says explicitly that he tells it not as mysticism but as illustration: when the gas quieted the grossest layers of processing, buddhi briefly reflected at a subtler level. The everyday solid world is not a lie, just not the whole picture. Kai calls it buddhi’s low-resolution setting.",
 "An illustration, not a proof","Part 3"),
mc("A","James’s thought “this class always switches off on me” fires. What does the practical experiment ask him to do?",
 ["Catch it as a verdict, not a fact, overgeneralised from a yawn: is it true, always, what else.","Accept it as a fact, so as to plan his lessons around a class that has made up its mind about him.","Push it from his mind at once, so as to teach with no doubt in himself and no thought of the class.","Ask the class to say what they think of him, so as to know for certain whether the thought is true."],0,
 "He is to notice whether teaching from let me re-engage them lands better than teaching from they have rejected me. Sarah is to catch the verdict, ask the three questions, then check reality by simply asking her son. Kai is to find the middle: I struggled with this part today.",
 "Treat the thought as a hypothesis","Practical situation")])
